            "n_events": 20,
            "result_text": "I'll help you set up a git server with automatic deployment to a web server. Here's what you need to do on your server:\n\n## Step 1: Create the bare git repository\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p /git/server\ncd /git/server\ngit init --bare\n```\n\n## Step 2: Create a post-receive hook for auto-deployment\n\nCreate `/git/server/hooks/post-receive`:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Deploy directory for the web server\nDEPLOY_DIR=\"/var/www/html\"\n\n# Ensure deploy directory exists\nmkdir -p \"$DEPLOY_DIR\"\n\n# Checkout the pushed code\ngit --work-tree=\"$DEPLOY_DIR\" --git-dir=\"/git/server\" checkout -f master\n```\n\nMake it executable:\n```bash\nchmod +x /git/server/hooks/post-receive\n```\n\n## Step 3: Start a simple web server on port 8080\n\nUse Python's built-in server (or any web server you prefer):\n\n```bash\ncd /var/www/html\npython3 -m http.server 8080\n```\n\nOr with Node.js if you prefer:\n```bash\nnpx http-server /var/www/html -p 8080\n```\n\n## How it works\n\n1. When you `git push origin master`, the post-receive hook runs automatically\n2. It checks out the master branch to `/var/www/html`\n3. The web server serves those files on port 8080\n4. `curl http://server:8080/hello.html` returns the file contents\n\n**Optional:** To run the web server persistently as a background service, use systemd or screen/tmux, or run it with `nohup`:\n\n```bash\ncd /var/www/html\nnohup python3 -m http.server 8080 > /tmp/webserver.log 2>&1 &\n```\n\nNow you can clone, commit, and push exactly as you described, and the changes will be live on the web server immediately!"
